+\AuthorResponse{We thank the reviewer for this important point and address each part below. The rolling-origin evaluation described here was added in response to Reviewer~2 (Major Comment~5); it is reported again here because it also addresses this comment, and no additional experiment was performed for this response.
 
-\ChangeLocation
+\textbf{Length of the main training period.} We agree that the main 2004--2021 split provides a limited training history. Its training targets span 2 January 2004 to 7 August 2016, approximately 12.6 years, and its test period covers less than three years (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset}). These main results, including Table~\ref{manuscript-tab:seed_stability}, are retained on the original split so that they remain consistent with the other analyses in the paper.
+
+\textbf{Longer training histories.} The supplementary evaluation uses an extended ERA5 archive for the same domain and variables covering 30 years (1992--2021) (Sections~\ref{manuscript-sec:rolling_origin_methods} and~\ref{manuscript-sec:rolling_origin_results}; Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r2m5_design}--\ref{supp-stab:r2m5_coverage}). Four expanding-window folds use training histories of 16, 19, 22 and 25 years, each followed by a two-year validation period and a three-year test period. In the longest-history fold, 25 years (1992--2016) are used for model fitting. The remaining five years were reserved for chronologically separated validation and testing, rather than being used for model fitting. Models were therefore not trained on all 30 years. Six models were trained with five fixed seeds, giving 150 runs in total. A matched fold trained on 2004--2016 (13 calendar years) shares the validation and test targets of the 25-year fold. On these identical targets, extending the training history from 13 to 25 years reduced the six learning models' all-target RMSE by approximately 0.4--2.3\%. The seed-based and test-sample bootstrap intervals for this comparison are reported separately (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m5_history}), and upper-tail gains were not uniform.
+
+\textbf{Length of the test period.} The four non-overlapping three-year test periods together cover twelve consecutive calendar years (2010--2021). In every test period, all six learning models retained positive RMSE skill relative to training-fitted month-by-hour climatology (0.077--0.125), although the model with the lowest RMSE varied between periods (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:rolling_origin_results}; Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m5_performance}).
+
+\textbf{Limitations and ENSO.} This evaluation is retrospective rather than a fully independent prospective validation: the development data of the original study overlap the earlier rolling test years, and the two earliest folds have fewer than 20 training years (16 and 19). Although the test periods span twelve consecutive years, we did not stratify the evaluation by ENSO phase or examine teleconnection effects on regional precipitation skill. These limitations are stated in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:limitations}, and we do not claim that the influence of ENSO on forecast skill has been assessed.}
+
+\ManuscriptChange{Existing Sections~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset}, \ref{manuscript-sec:rolling_origin_methods}, \ref{manuscript-sec:rolling_origin_results} and~\ref{manuscript-sec:limitations}; Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r2m5_design}--\ref{supp-stab:r2m5_coverage}. These were added in response to Reviewer~2 (Major Comment~5); no manuscript, supplementary or figure change was made for this response. Final page and line numbers pending full-manuscript assembly.}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Main split and its training period (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The main 2004--2021 experiment contains 18,410/3,945/3,945 raw training/validation/test timestamps and 18,404/3,939/3,939 forecast targets after six-snapshot windows are constructed separately within each partition. The target-time ranges (UTC) are 2 January 2004 12:00--7 August 2016 06:00 for training, 9 August 2016 00:00--20 April 2019 12:00 for validation, and 22 April 2019 06:00--31 December 2021 18:00 for testing. These are forecast target times, not input-window start times. The 1992--2003 extension is used only in the supplementary rolling-origin evaluation.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Rolling-origin folds and training histories (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:rolling_origin_methods})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{We conducted a retrospective rolling-origin robustness evaluation using the extended 1992--2021 ERA5 archive. Expanding training periods were 1992--2007, 1992--2010, 1992--2013 and 1992--2016; their validation periods were 2008--2009, 2011--2012, 2014--2015 and 2017--2018, and their non-overlapping test periods were 2010--2012, 2013--2015, 2016--2018 and 2019--2021, respectively (F1--F4). F4$\prime$ instead trained on 2004--2016 and shared F4\textquotesingle s validation and test targets, isolating the addition of 1992--2003 training history within this protocol. The longest training history was 25 years; F1 and F2 used 16 and 19 years. Six models and five fixed seeds (42, 123, 7, 2024, 99) yielded 150 runs. The six models were XGBoost, Transformer, BiLSTM-Attention, BU-LSTM, BU-LSTM-Attention and Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention; the plain BiLSTM control was not included.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Matched 13- versus 25-year training histories (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:rolling_origin_results})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{On matched F4/F4$\prime$ test targets, extending training from 13 to 25 years reduced the six learning models\textquotesingle{} mean all-target RMSE by approximately 0.4--2.3\%. Upper-tail gains were not uniform, and seed-$t$ and test-sample bootstrap intervals did not always agree; both are retained. Conditional 95\% Gaussian coverage across F1--F4 was 0.953--0.963 overall, but only 0.801--0.828 for wet targets and 0.473--0.592 for upper-tail targets. Under the fixed Q95 rule, every learning model\textquotesingle s mean CSI was lower than Persistence in each period. The coverage and fixed-event diagnostics use fold-specific training Q95, whereas the history comparison uses the common original-main Q95 $=0.574048$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ for upper-tail targets. Fixed-cutoff event scores are distinct from the validation-selected decisions reported elsewhere.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Retrospective design, short early folds and ENSO (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:limitations})}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The rolling-origin analysis is retrospective rather than a fully prospective or fully independent validation of model-design choices. The original training targets extended through 7 August 2016 and validation targets through 20 April 2019; these development data overlap the F1--F3 test years. F4 begins on 2 January 2019, so its early-2019 targets also overlap the original validation period; the original test targets begin only on 22 April 2019. The original test portion was not used for initial hyperparameter selection but had already informed the original model comparisons. Fold-specific fitting and preprocessing therefore do not remove development-stage overlap. Models were not retuned per fold; F1/F2 had only 16/19 training years. No ENSO-phase stratification, rolling plain-BiLSTM control or rolling Bernoulli--Gamma alternative was evaluated. Pooled bootstrap blocks can cross the 42-hour gaps between adjacent fold target sequences. These boundaries limit claims of temporal independence, attention-specific benefit and robustness to distributional formulation.}
+}
 
